[{:db/id -1
  :adr/id "adr-2809070300-consensus-witness-signer-custody"
  :adr/title "Custody and identity of the warrant witness signer for consensus fork evidence"
  :adr/status "proposed"
  :adr/date "2026-09-07"
  :adr/related ["ADR-2809060410-fork-evidence-warrant-seam" "ADR-2809052250-effect-consistency-classification-draft"]
  :adr/context
  "Landing path (b) of ADR-2809060410 wires consensus_order fork detection to emit nekko.warrant envelopes. kotoba-lang/src/kotoba/lang/consensus_order.cljc admit-commit! detects :consensus/order-invalid and already carries recoverable :consensus/candidate {:height :parent-id :commit-id} + :consensus/observed-height + :consensus/expected-parent in the exception ex-data (seq 12/13, PR #614 merged). nekko.warrant/issue (origin/main 988ccef) signs that evidence with an Ed25519 seed (did:key envelope) and returns an inert warrant. The open question is WHERE the witness Ed25519 seed lives. This is not a free choice: consensus_order is a Q9-pure decision function under the Kotoba safe-application-language rules (no ambient authority, no raw credential in the decision core, capability-provider boundary for effects), and the throw site has no key in scope by design. Any answer that puts a raw seed inside consensus_order would violate the capability model this stack is being built on."
  :adr/decision
  "1. The witness seed is NEVER stored in or reachable from the pure decision path (consensus_order.admit-commit!). It is enrolled once at sealed-order bootstrap and held only by a host-side witness capability (the Kotoba :cap/kind host boundary, analogue of the fs/cap / secret/get capability ports, NOT a field on the consensus envelope or a var in kotoba-lang/src). 2. consensus_order stays a faithful pure function: it continues to THROW :consensus/order-invalid carrying the recoverable evidence. It does not call nekko.warrant/issue. 3. The emission seam is the host wrapper that catches :consensus/order-invalid at the event-admission boundary (the caller of apply-commit/admit-commit! that owns the validated ingestion), where the witness capability is in scope. The wrapper extracts :consensus/candidate from the ex-data and calls nekko.warrant/issue with: :warrant/kind OrderInvalid, rid = dataspace, ref = observed parent ref, event = the candidate map. 4. Enroll/rotation of the witness did:key is a sealed-order bootstrap concern (the same place the Inga QC / quorum cert mints), not a per-commit decision. A warrant is signed by the witnessing peer, so each replica's witness key is its own; there is no shared secret. 5. This custody split is the compiler consequence of ADR-2809052250's :consistency classes: :validated-claim disputes reference a warrant, and a warrant is by construction produced where the capability is bound, matching the :countersigned / :inga-qc boundary from the distributed-authority threat model."
  :adr/consequences
  "The fork-evidence path is now fully designed but code lands in two verifiable slices: (i) this decision, and (ii) the wrapper + nekko git-dep wiring, which must pass amu compile --jvm-free before landing (Q9). Multiple peers observing the same fork each emit their own warrant with their own did:key, so fork evidence is naturally multi-signed and attributable without a shared secret. Risk: the host wrapper seam (who exactly catches :consensus/order-invalid in production) is not yet named in code - that is the next survey before slice (ii). No code changed under this ADR."}]
